    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d117">

                <pb facs="0246" n="228" sameAs="frus:frus1946v01/pg228"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source"> IO Files: US/A/152</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum of Conversation, by Randolph Harrison of the
                        United States Delegation Staff of Advisers</hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">New
                                York</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian" when="1946-11-20"
                            >November 20, 1946</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <table>
                    <row role="label">
                        <cell rendition="#top #nowrap">Participants:</cell>
                        <cell>Mr. Fack of the Netherlands Delegation</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell/>
                        <cell>Mr. van Roijen, of the Netherlands Delegation</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell/>
                        <cell>Mr. Randolph Harrison</cell>
                    </row>
                </table>
                <p>Both Mr. van Roijen and Mr. Fack, of the Netherlands Delegation, sought me out to
                    express their deep concern and disappointment over the action of the United
                    States in withdrawing its support from the Netherlands for a seat on the ECOSOC.
                    Mr. Fack said that losing the vote of the United States was bad enough in
                    itself, but since the act of the United States was generally known due to
                    newspaper publicity, he felt that it would undoubtedly influence other nations
                    to withdraw their support from the Netherlands. Mr. van Roijen and Mr. Fack both
                    expressed the earnest hope that the decision of the United States on this
                    subject was not final and that it would reinstate the Netherlands in its
                    slate.</p>
            </div>
